    "result_text": "All 287 tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field on the owner-creation response following the established pattern used by the prior `namesakeCount`/`sharesHousehold` features.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema (plain string, matching `customerCode` \u2014 not an enum, to avoid a MapStruct `valueOf` mismatch on the lowercase values).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `locality` column field with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`** \u2014 Added `owner.setLocality(resolveLocality(owner))` in the new-owner branch, plus the `resolveLocality` helper: it groups existing owners by city (case-insensitive), finds the max count, and returns `\"local\"` only when a **single** city uniquely has that max and it matches the new owner's city; otherwise `\"remote\"` (also `\"remote\"` for a null city, no existing owners, or a tie for most common).\n\n4. **Schema SQL** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added the `locality` column.\n\n5. **`hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 This seed file uses positional `INSERT INTO owners VALUES(...)`, so I appended the historically-correct locality per row (only David/Madison is `local`, since Madison was uniquely the most common city at 3 when he was added; all others `remote`), matching the existing convention where seed rows carry computed values.\n\nThe pre-existing acceptance test `Cp18Tests` (which reads `locality` back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`) now passes, confirming the field is persisted and returned correctly.",
